/*
  core/stressors/time_pressure.spthy -- f_U for sigma3 TimePressure, CEREMONY-AGNOSTIC.

  Migrated to the agnostic interface (AGNOSTIC_STRESSOR_INTERFACE.md). The old sigma3 pattern-matched
  a ceremony TASK NAME -- !Req(P,_,'CALC_SK',_,c) here, and a verbatim clone (time_pressure_share.spthy,
  now deleted) read !Req(P,_,'SHARE_PW',_,c). One psychological construct, two files, only because the
  trigger interface leaked the task name. This single detector instead reads:

    * !Step(P, sid, action)                  -- Layer 1: an OBJECTIVE interaction step the ceremony
                                                emits (action in the fixed taxonomy, never a task name)
    * !Demands(action,'TemporalDemand','hi') -- Layer 2: the usability verdict, from core/lexicon_tlx

  so it names no task. Both the KDF ceremony's CALC_SK and the email ceremony's password compose are
  'compute' steps the lexicon marks temporally demanding, so the ONE detector fires for both ceremonies.

  NASA-TLX Temporal Demand (Hart & Staveland 1988). At onset it emits SetMask(P,'Busy') -- everything
  below SetMask (mask_state.spthy gates, the masks, the outcomes) is UNCHANGED by the migration.
  !StressEnable(P) gates the onset per party (Stage B); OneTimePressure bounds it to one onset per party.
*/

rule Trigger_TimePressure:
    [ !Step(P, sid, action),
      !Demands(action, 'TemporalDemand', 'hi'),
      !StressEnable(P) ]
  --[ TimePressure(P), SetMask(P,'Busy') ]->
    [ ]

restriction OneTimePressure:
  "All p #i #j. TimePressure(p)@i & TimePressure(p)@j ==> #i = #j"
